<!-- BEGIN baseline:design-review v0.2.0 -->
## Portable Agent Baseline: Design Review

- Before re-implementing a persistence/failure-ordering decision a second time, confirm the tradeoff in prose first: write the causal sequence (call order, what data is available at each point, what breaks if reordered), get explicit confirmation, then implement once. A repeated "why is X needed" about a just-shipped design signals the tradeoff wasn't explained clearly, not a cue to immediately rebuild.
- When a new proposal reverses an established design principle, name what's being reversed before implementing it — state the concrete new risk it reopens and propose a mitigation if one exists, rather than complying silently. Only for a principle that was itself deliberate and documented.
- When a design claims to eliminate a risk "by construction," check two second-order failure modes before accepting the claim: (a) does the guarantee still hold if the mitigation/compensating action itself fails; (b) does the risk reset after one attempt or compound independently on every retry. Worth this scrutiny specifically for external, potentially irreversible side effects.
- Give each piece of state its own durability guarantee — a reusable cache (miss/staleness tolerable, self-heals) can be best-effort; a per-operation authoritative record should be atomic (complete or absent, never partial). Don't apply one uniform persistence policy to both by default.
- A fix found via one representation of a design (a summary diagram, a quick-reference doc) must be checked against every other parallel representation of the same design before considering it done — don't assume fixing the one you were shown is sufficient.
- A cancel/refund/rollback path must not unconditionally assume the forward action it's reversing actually completed — if the forward action has a conditional skip/partial-completion branch, the reversal needs its own explicit check for that specific side effect, not an inference from a coarser status flag.
- When two structurally similar flows are each supposed to enforce the same precondition, trace the actual call order in each and compare side by side — the same steps being present in both doesn't mean the enforcement order is the same; parallel implementations can silently drift.
- A sibling method's idempotency guard (or its absence) is evidence of design intent for whether an operation is safe to call twice — a guarded sibling and an unguarded target means the unguarded one likely wasn't built to tolerate repetition. Circumstantial evidence, not proof; confirm with the authoritative source before relying on it for anything with real consequences.
- Before judging a shape-consistency refactor's risk (widening a return type, adding a parameter to match a sibling), grep every implementer and call site and let the empirical count drive the call, not intuition — one implementer and one call site is low-risk almost by definition.
- When investigating a bug from a missing scoping invariant on a read endpoint, proactively check the corresponding write endpoint for the same defect — the write-side counterpart tends to be strictly more severe (corruption vs. a stale read) and easy to overlook once the read-path finding feels done.
- Derive a synthetic version key from a content hash, never a wall-clock timestamp or run identifier, when a source system provides no native version field — a timestamp always registers as "changed" independent of actual content drift.
- For a genuinely speculative, unbuilt design (nothing built yet to verify it against), offer 2-3 labeled options with an explicit tradeoff comparison and mark exactly one as recommended, rather than asserting a single answer as if already validated — a lone recommendation with nothing to check it against tends to get corrected repeatedly, one round at a time, instead of the alternatives being weighed once up front. Doesn't apply once a design is implemented/verified — converge to a single recommendation then.
- Reordering two workflow steps to eliminate a known failure risk (e.g. moving an irreversible external call to after a payment completes) generally trades that risk for a new, structurally different one, not eliminates it — explicitly name the new risk and design remediation for it, verified against the actual system's code/state machine, rather than presenting the reorder as simply "safer."

Apply this baseline when proposing, reviewing, or implementing a design decision, but never use it to override explicit user instructions, safety rules, privacy boundaries, or stricter repo-local instructions.
